          "distinct": true,
          "id": "d03ca659d4e409ef6d326c601c7aadc2f82d7302",
          "message": "fix(server): spec-conformant numeric/boolean TSV abbreviation, preserving data lexical forms (sq-u79ee, survey §C1 / FINDINGS F21) [OPUS-4.8] (#1258)\n\nThe SPARQL Results TSV serialiser (`select_to_tsv`) quoted EVERY typed literal,\ne.g. `\"30\"^^xsd:integer`, `\"1.0E6\"^^xsd:double` — non-conformant with the W3C\nQuery Results TSV format (`§ Encoding terms`) and the `csv-tsv-res` expected\nfiles (`csvtsv01`/`csvtsv03`), which write `xsd:integer`/`xsd:decimal`/\n`xsd:double`/`xsd:boolean` literals BARE as Turtle tokens (`30`, `2.2`, `1.0E6`,\n`true`) when their lexical form is a valid token.\n\nFix `term_to_tsv` to abbreviate exactly those four datatypes, writing the\nliteral's OWN lexical form (matching oxigraph's `sparesults` reference). This\nrealises the C1 contract at the serialiser:\n- DATA-sourced literals round-trip their original spelling (`\"1.0E6\"^^xsd:double`\n  → `1.0E6`, never canonicalised to `1.0e6`/`1000000`);\n- COMPUTED numerics already arrive from the engine in canonical form, so they\n  serialise canonically; a canonical integral double like `\"1000000\"^^xsd:double`\n  is NOT a valid Turtle DOUBLE token, so it stays quoted+typed — abbreviation\n  never silently changes a term's datatype;\n- integer/decimal SUBTYPES (`xsd:negativeInteger`) and custom datatypes stay\n  quoted+typed; `xsd:string` keeps its implicit-datatype short form.\n\nEmpirical scoping note (honest): the store does NOT normalise lexical forms on\nintern — a proven repro shows the N-Quads/Turtle load + projection path already\npreserves `1.0E6`→`1.0E6` and `1.0e6`→`1.0e6`, and a computed double already\nserialises canonical. So no `sparq-core` Dict change was needed (a preserved-\nlexical-form field would have been dead storage, zero memory cost incurred). The\nreal, narrow gap was the TSV writer's missing numeric abbreviation. CSV already\nwrites bare lexical values; JSON/XML carry the full term — both unaffected.\n\nThe W3C `tsv03` test remains a tracked DOCUMENTED_DIVERGENCE: its expected file\nwrites `1.0e6` for the data term `\"1.0E6\"^^xsd:double` (a different RDF term);\nthe conformance harness compares parsed terms (not serialised TSV), so this\nchange is conformance-neutral there (suite unchanged: 0 fail).\n\nTests: direct coverage of (i) data `1.0E6`→bare `1.0E6` (preserved, not\ncanonicalised), (ii) decimal/integer/boolean abbreviation + string/subtype/\ncustom-datatype staying quoted across the csvtsv03 corner cases, (iii) a\ncomputed double staying canonical (quoted+typed and bare-scientific cases),\n(iv) signed/leading-zero/leading-dot/exponent-required edge cases. The\ndifferential serializer oracle (re-parse via `sparesults`) stays lossless.\n\n🤖 SPARQ agent — gates green in both feature states (default + no-default-\nfeatures): build, clippy --all-targets -D warnings, test; rustdoc --workspace\n--all-features -D warnings clean; coverage-gate sparq-server 88.13% ≥ floor 86;\nMSRV 1.88 (crate + workspace) ok; typos/terminology/privacy-claims/readme-\ntemplate clean; memmap2 pinned 0.9.11.\n\nCo-authored-by: Jesse Wright <jmwright.045@gmail.com>\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-06-24T03:48:16Z",
          "tree_id": "1ef2e083baf54a153b6ef7becdefc13d11e30cee",
